/*!
    \page guide-conventions.html
    \title Coding Conventions
    \previouspage {Architecture}
    \nextpage {Recipes}

    \section1 C++

    \list
        \li Everything in the core uses \c snake_case.
        \li Classes, structs, enums and type aliases end with \c _t, for example \l game_t
            or \c position_t.
        \li Function parameters end with \c _p and data members end with \c _.
        \li The core must not include Qt headers. If you need Qt, the code belongs in the bridge.
        \li Numbers a designer might want to change go to \c data, not into the code.
        \li New logic in the core comes with a GoogleTest test when it can be tested in isolation.
    \endlist

    \section1 Qt bridge and QML

    \list
        \li The bridge and everything QML sees use the usual Qt \c camelCase.
        \li Import Qt modules without a version, for example \c {import QtQuick}. A versioned
            import hides newer properties.
        \li Add \c {pragma ComponentBehavior: Bound} to files where a delegate, \c Loader
            component or \c layer.effect uses ids from outside.
        \li Do not name your own properties like built in ones. \c Item already has \c left,
            \c top and similar anchor lines, and redefining them fails when the file loads.
        \li Inside a scaled \c Scene give sizes in scene units with \c font.pixelSize and
            \c size. Felgo's \c sp() and \c dp() would scale a second time on phones.
    \endlist

    \section1 Comments

    Code should explain itself through names and structure. When something really is not
    obvious, a comment of a few words is enough, one sentence at most. Longer explanations
    belong in this documentation.

    \section1 Graphics

    \list
        \li Everything uses the oblique projection: an object stands on its tiles anchored at
            the bottom right, height goes up and to the left at 45 degrees.
        \li The light comes from the top left. Top faces are the brightest, east faces darker,
            south faces the darkest.
        \li One pixel of art is one scene unit. A tile is 16 by 16, a creature frame 32 by 32.
        \li Every image has a base version, a \c +hd version at twice the size and a \c +hd2
            version at four times the size, scaled without smoothing.
        \li Sprite sheets use TexturePacker JSON (Hash) with frames named
            \c {<direction>_<index>.png}, rows in the order north, south, west, east, one pixel
            of extrude and two pixels of padding.
    \endlist

    \section1 Commits

    Write commit messages in English with a short title and a few lines about what changed
    and why. The history is public, so it should read well to someone new to the project.
*/
